google/embeddinggemma-2 and are covered by the same license and attribution.


MODIFICATIONS (Apache License, Section 4(b))
--------------------------------------------

Changes made to google/embeddinggemma-2, 11-12 October 2026, to produce model/embeddinggemma-2-text-mm.aimodel,
model/embeddinggemma-2-vision.aimodel and model/embeddinggemma-2-audio.aimodel.

Text encoder (v1.0, unchanged in v1.1 and v1.2 except where noted):

  2026-10-11  The audio encoder of the multimodal checkpoint was dropped (v1.0 also dropped the vision
              encoder; v1.1 adds the vision encoder back and v1.2 the audio encoder, see below).
  2026-10-11  The encoder architecture (24 bidirectional Gemma 4 layers, per-layer embeddings, RMSNorm with
              max-abs rescaling, RoPE, GQA/MQA attention, GELU-tanh MLP, final RMSNorm, 512->768 projection,
              masked mean pooling, L2 normalization) was re-authored in plain PyTorch for the Core AI
              converter. The weights are the original ones rounded from bfloat16 to float16, except that the
              sqrt(512) token-embedding scale and the 512^-0.5 per-layer-embedding scale are folded into the
              tables.
  2026-10-11  fp16 handling for the Neural Engine: every RMSNorm and the final L2 normalization divide the row
              by its max-abs value before squaring (activations reach ~2000, whose square overflows fp16);
              in the model code every division is expressed as rsqrt(x)*rsqrt(x); the additive attention-mask
              value is -10000. (The model card advises against float16; this rescaling is what makes fp16
              safe here, see the parity figures in README.md.)
  2026-10-11  Pooling, projection and normalization run inside the graph; the output is the final embedding.
  2026-10-11  Fixed sequence buckets, one function per bucket. Input longer than 2048 tokens is not supported
              by this build (the original accepts 8192).
  2026-10-12  Attention of the four global (full-attention, single key/value head) layers rewritten from a
              5-D grouped matmul to an equivalent plain 4-D matmul with the key/value tensors broadcast over
              the four query heads, so that the Neural Engine compiler accepts the whole graph. Mathematically
              identical to the reference (fp32 max abs diff 3e-7).

Added in v1.1:

  2026-10-12  Text: one input mix at the start of the graph, x = embed(input_ids) * (1 - mm_mask) +
              mm_embeds * mm_mask, so that externally computed image soft tokens replace the <|image|>
              placeholder embeddings. Soft tokens are not scaled; everything after the mix is the v1.0 text
              graph, and with zero soft tokens the outputs are bit-identical to v1.0. The functions are now
              mm_<S> for S in 32, 64, 128, 288, 512, 1024, 2048 (288 replaces 256 so that one image sequence of
              at most 284 tokens fits). This asset replaces v1.0's model/embeddinggemma-2-text.aimodel.
  2026-10-12  Vision encoder (Gemma 4 vision model: patch embedder, 16 bidirectional encoder layers with axial
              2-D RoPE, 3x3 average pooling) and the vision embedder (RMSNorm + 768->512 projection) re-authored
              in plain PyTorch for the Core AI converter. The weights are the original ones rounded from
              bfloat16 to float16, with the scale fold and table cut described in the next entries (the 2/255
              input scale folded into the patch-embedding projection, the position tables cut to 840 rows).
  2026-10-12  Vision: one fixed shape, the default budget of 280 soft tokens (2520 patches of 16x16 px). The
              patches are fed in "cell-major" order (the 9 patches of each 3x3 pooling cell contiguous, cells
              row-major, padding last) so that the pooling is a reshape and a mean instead of a one-hot matmul.
              Attention is permutation-equivariant, so the result is identical.
  2026-10-12  Vision: the pooler's multiplication by sqrt(768) (computed in float32 upstream because it exceeds
              the float16 range) is dropped and the vision embedder's RMSNorm uses eps/768 instead of eps:
              RMSNorm(x*s, eps) == RMSNorm(x, eps/s^2) exactly.
  2026-10-12  Vision: the 2-D position-embedding tables (2 x 10240 x 768) are cut to the 840 rows that an image
              of this budget can address; the axial-RoPE cos/sin tables and the key mask are inputs computed by
              the host instead of being computed in the graph.
  2026-10-12  Vision fp16 handling for the Neural Engine: every RMSNorm divides the row by its max-abs value
              before squaring (the vision residual reaches ~1400), divisions are expressed as rsqrt(x)*rsqrt(x),
              the additive key-mask value is -10000, the 3x3 mean is a sum times 1/9. The attention queries are
              processed in 12 blocks (same result, lower Neural Engine latency).
  2026-10-12  Vision input scaling: the model takes integer pixel values 0..255 (exact in float16) instead of
              pixel/255. The graph computes P - 127.5 and the 2/255 factor is folded into the weights of the
              patch-embedding projection, which equals upstream's projection of 2*(P/255) - 1 without rounding
              the inputs to float16 (that rounding cost heavily upscaled tiny images up to 0.004 of cosine).
  2026-10-12  Image preprocessing (host side, not part of the model): the upstream image processor's
              aspect-preserving size rule and its antialiased bicubic resize on 8-bit pixels (the ATen uint8
              kernel used by torchvision) re-implemented in numpy and in Swift so that the pixels fed to the
              model are identical to the reference pipeline.

Added in v1.2:

  2026-10-12  Audio encoder (Gemma 4 audio model: two stride-2 subsampling convolutions with channel LayerNorm,
              a 1024->1024 projection, 12 conformer layers of half-step feed-forward, local causal attention with
              relative-position bias, light depthwise convolution, half-step feed-forward and RMSNorm, the
              1024->1536 output projection) and the audio embedder (RMSNorm without scale + 1536->512 projection)
              re-authored in plain PyTorch for the Core AI converter. The weights are the original ones rounded
              from bfloat16 to float16, with the scale folds listed below.
  2026-10-12  Audio attention written in its banded form: the upstream chunked local attention (chunks of 12,
              13 of left context, pad / unfold / rel-shift) lets every token see itself and the 11 previous
              tokens; the port computes exactly those 12 logits per token, logit[t, d] = q_t . (k_{t-d} +
              relk_d), where relk_d = relative_k_proj(sinusoid(d)) is precomputed per layer as a constant. The
              query scale (128^-0.5 / ln 2 times softplus(per_dim_scale)) and the key scale (ln(1 + e) / ln 2)
              are folded into q_proj / k_proj and their output clip bounds. The masked_fill(-1e9) of padded keys
              is an additive -10000; the gradient-clipping clamps (+-1e10, no-ops in inference) are dropped; the
              120 clipped linears keep their clip bounds. Mathematically identical to the reference (fp32
              soft-token cosine >= 0.99999998 on 213 clips, max abs diff 8e-4).
  2026-10-12  Audio fp16 handling for the Neural Engine: every RMSNorm (including the scale-free one of the
              audio embedder) divides the row by its max-abs value before squaring (the residual stream reaches
              ~412), divisions are expressed as rsqrt(x)*rsqrt(x), the logit softcap is tanh(x/50)*50.
  2026-10-12  Audio: fixed frame buckets 192, 384, 1024, 1536 and 3000 frames of 10 ms (functions audio_<Tb>);
              inputs longer than 30 s are truncated by the host, as upstream does by default. The three masks
              (frames, after the first convolution, tokens) are host inputs. The bucket set is limited to the
              sizes validated on the Neural Engine.
  2026-10-12  Audio preprocessing (host side, not part of the model): the upstream feature extractor's log-mel
              computation (30 s truncation, 160-sample left pad, 320-sample periodic Hann frames every 160
              samples, 512-point magnitude FFT, 128 HTK mel bands 0-8000 Hz without normalization, ln(mel +
              0.001), frame validity rule) re-implemented in numpy and in Swift; the mono mix (mean of the
              channels) as librosa does it; resampling to 16 kHz with a Kaiser-windowed sinc low-pass of our own
              (pass band 0.913 and stop band 1.0 of the lower Nyquist, 125 dB) instead of the libsoxr "HQ"
              resampler that librosa.load uses upstream (the only intended deviation from upstream in the audio
              pipeline; README gives the measured difference).

  2026-10-11/12  Converted with coreai-torch 0.4.3 / coreai-core 1.0.0b3 on torch 2.9.0 to the Core AI
              .aimodel format (macOS 27).

The exporter in export/ reproduces these changes from the original checkpoint.


